Found: [oath, eat]
```

### Why Is the Trie Approach Better Than Per-Word Search?

Without a Trie, we would run a separate DFS for each word in the dictionary, repeating enormous amounts of work. With a Trie, shared prefixes are tested together in a single grid sweep. If words "oath" and "oat" share the prefix "oa", the Trie explores that prefix only once.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class TrieVsPerWord {
    // Per-word approach: O(W * m * n * 4^L) where W = number of words
    // Trie approach: O(m * n * 4^L) total, shared prefixes tested once
    // For W = 1000 words, the Trie approach is orders of magnitude faster
}
```

### How Does the Trie Construction Work?

We insert every dictionary word into a single Trie. Each node has 26 children for lowercase English letters. At the terminal node of each word, we store the complete word string so collection is immediate without reconstructing the word from the path.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class TrieConstruction {
    static class TrieNode {
        TrieNode[] children = new TrieNode[26];
        String word = null;
    }

    public static TrieNode buildTrie(String[] words) {
        TrieNode root = new TrieNode();
        for (String w : words) {
            TrieNode node = root;
            for (char c : w.toCharArray()) {
                int idx = c - 'a';
                if (node.children[idx] == null) {
                    node.children[idx] = new TrieNode();
                }
                node = node.children[idx];
            }
            node.word = w;  // store complete word for O(1) collection
        }
        return root;
    }
}
```

### How Does the DFS Walk the Trie?

The DFS carries a Trie pointer alongside the grid position. Before entering a cell, it checks whether the cell's character is a child of the current Trie node. If not, the entire branch is pruned because no dictionary word has this prefix. This is the key optimization.

```java
