### How Is This Different from Word Search?

| Aspect | Word Search | Word Search II |
|---|---|---|
| Input | One word | List of words |
| Data structure | None | Trie for all words |
| Grid sweeps | One per starting cell per word | One total sweep |
| Pruning | Character mismatch | Trie prefix (much stronger) |
| Output | Boolean (found/not found) | List of found words |
| Duplicate prevention | Not needed | Null out word field after collection |

### What Are Common Mistakes?

The first mistake is running a separate DFS for each word without a Trie, which is correct but too slow for large dictionaries. The second is forgetting to null out the word field after collection, which causes the same word to be added multiple times.

```java
// WRONG: word collected multiple times
if (node.word != null) {
    result.add(node.word);
    // BUG: node.word still set, will be collected again from another path
}

// CORRECT: clear after collection
if (node.word != null) {
    result.add(node.word);
    node.word = null;  // prevent duplicate collection
}
```

### What About Trie Leaf Pruning?

An advanced optimization is to prune Trie leaf nodes after their word has been collected. If a Trie node has no children and its word is null, it serves no purpose and can be removed. This shrinks the Trie as the search proceeds, making later DFS calls faster.

